    "scenario": "MPLS L3VPN VRF and VPNv4 Basics",
    "goals": "• {{router2_name}}, {{router3_name}} and {{router4_name}} form an MPLS backbone where OSPF area 0, the Loopback0 addresses and LDP are already configured and operational on the two core links {{link2_cidr}} and {{link3_cidr}}.\n    customer site A: {{router1_name}}, Loopback1 {{router1_loopback1_cidr}}, attached to {{router2_name}} over {{link1_cidr}}\n    MPLS backbone: {{router2_name}} (PE), {{router3_name}} (P), {{router4_name}} (PE)\n    customer site B: {{router5_name}}, Loopback1 {{router5_loopback1_cidr}}, attached to {{router4_name}} over {{link4_cidr}}\n• The two customer routers only have a default route towards their PE. The PE interfaces facing them are addressed but still belong to the global routing table.\n• Create the VPN routing instance {{vrf_name}} on {{router2_name}} and {{router4_name}} with route distinguisher {{rd}}, and with {{rt}} as both the exported and the imported route target.\n• Move the CE-facing interface of each PE into {{vrf_name}} while keeping its current address ({{link1_ip2}} on {{router2_name}}, {{link4_ip1}} on {{router4_name}}).\n• Build one iBGP VPNv4 session between the two PE Loopback0 addresses ({{router2_loopback0_ip}} and {{router4_loopback0_ip}}) in AS {{as_number}}, sourced from Loopback0, and make sure extended communities are exchanged on it.\n• Inside {{vrf_name}} reach each customer network with a static route towards the local CE and advertise those routes into the VPN.\n• Result: {{router1_name}} must reach {{router5_loopback1_cidr}} and {{router5_name}} must reach {{router1_loopback1_cidr}}. The customer prefixes must stay out of the global routing table of the PEs.",
